  "term": "growl",
  "says": "The dubstep / neuro growl: a midrange bass whose character is a snarling, vowel-like motion of the harmonics rather than a level wobble. The oscillator is frequency-modulated by the sub (osc_1 FM'd by osc_2, a sine at the fundamental) so the spectrum is dense above the fundamental; a resonant filter (dual-notch or band-pass, resonance 0.6-0.8) carves formants; one LFO sweeps the filter and a second, slower one moves the wavetable frame; a hard clip after the filter for grit; the sine sub goes around the filter to keep the low end clean. Unison stays at one or two voices - the fat comes from drive, not detune. Signature: a low fundamental, a centroid in the low mids (well above a reese's, below a lead's), a brightness that moves by semitones at an LFO rate, harmonics that stay mostly on the harmonic grid, and more energy above 2.5 kHz than a reese has. Measured 2026-09-14: the entry's patch reads centroid 1670 Hz, brightness moving by 12.5 semitones at 4.0 Hz (the filter LFO), harshness -14 dB against the reese's -52, harmonicity 0.97. The first pitch detector (YIN on the loudest 100 ms, absolute threshold) declared it unvoiced: the swept resonance rings louder than the fundamental and is periodic at no lag. The detector now reads several windows on a low-passed copy with YIN's global-minimum fallback; it reports the period (90.3 Hz) where the sub's spectral peak sits at 87.5 - the FM'd harmonics carry the LFO's phase modulation.",
